<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

Lesson 17 gave you verdicts. An auditor asks two different questions: **is this subject compliant right now**, and **what exactly did the engine decide last Tuesday at 11:00, on which facts, under which rules?** `ash_compliance` answers both with two artifacts that must never be conflated:

* **Finding**: a projection (lesson 16) per `[organization_id, control_id, subject_type, subject_id]`. The "now". It changes.
* **ComplianceEvaluation**: append-only, pinning the bundle hash, the fact snapshot and the outcome. The "why". Nothing rewrites it.

Both come from the same projector, in the same transaction.

## Rule changes are events, not edits

Rules move through a lifecycle as resources: a `RuleSetRevision` goes `draft → validated → approved → active → retired/revoked`. Tenants tailor through profiles and waivers, compiled with a **fixed precedence**: non-waivable global > global mandatory > profile refinements > tenant strengthening > approved overrides/waivers > tenant supplements. The compiler refuses overreach and weakening. The output is an immutable, SHA-256 hashed `PolicyBundle`, the only thing that evaluates.

Register `Tutorial.RefundRules` (lesson 17; move it to `lib/ash_enterprise/tutorial/refund_rules.ex`) as a global mandatory revision for your organization, take it to active, compile and activate a bundle. Everything goes through the `AshCompliance.Domain` code interfaces.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4"></i>Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  bundle_src = Tutorial.RefundRules.__bundle__()

  revision =
    AshCompliance.Domain.draft_rule_set_revision!(%{
      organization_id: tenant,
      name: "refund-baseline",
      layer: :global_mandatory,
      rules_json: AshRules.Ir.encode!(bundle_src),
      content_hash: bundle_src.content_hash
    }, actor: actor)

  revision
  |> AshCompliance.Domain.validate_rule_set_revision!(actor: actor)
  |> AshCompliance.Domain.approve_rule_set_revision!(actor: actor)
  |> AshCompliance.Domain.activate_rule_set_revision!(actor: actor)

  {:ok, bundle} = AshCompliance.Domain.compile_policy_bundle(%{organization_id: tenant}, actor: actor)
  AshCompliance.Domain.activate_policy_bundle!(bundle, actor: actor)
  ```

  Note `validate → approve → activate`. Validation happens before activation, never at evaluation: a bundle that reaches production with a broken rule has defeated the design.

  </div>
</details>

## Project findings from events

Copy the shape of `AshEnterprise.Compliance.Projector`:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
defmodule AshEnterprise.Tutorial.RefundComplianceProjector do
  use AshCompliance.Projector,
    name: "tutorial_refund_findings_v1",
    event_log: AshEnterprise.Compliance.EventLog,
    projection_resource: AshCompliance.Resources.Finding,
    bundle: {AshEnterprise.Compliance.Projector, :active_bundle, []}

  grain fn event ->
    m = event.metadata || %{}
    %{organization_id: m["organization_id"], control_id: m["control_id"],
      subject_type: "refund", subject_id: event.record_id}
  end

  project_all [:refund_approved]
end
```

Your `RecordRefundEvent` change (lesson 16) must now also put the **facts** in the event (`amount_over_limit`, `manager_approved`, `customer_tier`, with string subjects, because this bundle crossed the wire) and a `control_id` in metadata. The projector hydrates facts from the event, never by querying live state: replaying the log must reproduce the same findings.

Approve a refund over the limit without manager approval, then read both artifacts for it.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4"></i>Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  require Ash.Query

  AshCompliance.Resources.Finding
  |> Ash.Query.filter(subject_id == ^refund.id)
  |> Ash.read!(actor: actor, tenant: tenant)

  AshCompliance.Resources.ComplianceEvaluation
  |> Ash.Query.filter(subject_id == ^refund.id)
  |> Ash.read!(actor: actor, tenant: tenant)
  |> Enum.map(&{&1.outcome, &1.bundle_hash})
  ```

  Approve a second time after fixing it: the Finding flips to resolved, and the evaluation log now has two rows. That is the difference between "now" and "why".

  </div>
</details>

## Waivers are bounded

A `PolicyOverride` (waiver) carries a time bound, an approver and compensating controls, and cannot outrank a non-waivable global. "The tenant tweaked the rule" is not a thing; a waiver is a reviewable record. If you want approval of the waiver itself, that is lesson 20.

